# Line endings, settled once.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its SYSTEM config, so on this
# machine the working tree gets CRLF while the repository is expected to hold
# LF. Without the rules below that expectation is only a habit: seven files had
# drifted to CRLF *in the repository*, which makes `git diff` report a whole-file
# rewrite the moment anything touches one — the real change buried under two
# hundred lines of invisible noise.
#
# `text=auto` puts LF in the repository for everything Git considers text, while
# leaving the working tree to each platform's own convention. The exceptions
# below are the files where the working tree's convention is not a preference.

* text=auto

# A batch file with LF line endings can fail in cmd.exe, notably around
# multi-line constructs. This one is Gradle's own wrapper.
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must be LF even when checked out on Windows: bash reads the
# trailing CR as part of the command and reports things like
# `$'\r': command not found`, which names neither the file nor the cause.
# These run under Git Bash here and would run under a normal shell elsewhere.
*.sh            text eol=lf
gradlew         text eol=lf

# Binary. Declared rather than detected, so a stray NUL-free header can never
# get one of these normalised.
*.png           binary
*.jar           binary
*.der           binary
*.apk           binary
*.jks           binary
*.keystore      binary
*.zip           binary
*.so            binary

# Not a secret, and not to be mangled: the OTA manifest's public key, in PEM.
# Text, but its bytes are hashed by tools/release.sh GATE-3 against the DER
# copy, so it stays LF everywhere.
*.pem           text eol=lf

# GitHub's language statistics otherwise call this a Python project, because
# tools/ is larger in bytes than the Kotlin it supports.
tools/*.py      linguist-language=Python linguist-detectable=true
docs/*.md       linguist-documentation
